how do many kinds of cells arise from a  single cell during development single  cell sequencing technologies and new  computational tools are providing the  most detailed picture yet of this  process in several types of studies  embryos of different stages were  separated into individual cells and the  cells are sequestered away in droplets  of oil what kind of cell am I looking at  here to find out  the researchers sequenced each cell's  messenger RNA which reflects active  genes each cell type has a profile set  of active genes that define its identity  as a muscle cell or a type of brain cell  how do I track a cell through time in  one line of experiments  researchers analyzed some 92,000  zebrafish cells and gathered data from  seven different embryo stages to help  tie the cells to a time point they had a  genetic barcode DNA to the cytoplasm of  some of the single-cell embryos these  are many tiny pieces of unique DNA that  will be incorporated over time into the  genomes of daughter cells by the end of  the experiment  the combinations of barcodes reveal how  the cells are related thus they could  trace each zebrafish cells fate through  time to see how a single cell gave rise  to a variety of specialized cells such  as heart nerves and skin what can this  tell us  this approach is already yielding  results developmental biologists have  thought that once the cell starts down a  path to become say a muscle cell that it  would not stray but researchers observed  some zebrafish cells switch to different  new types as indicated by the change in  what genes were active after they should  have been well committed to a certain  faith in frogs gene activities showed  that even when an embryo looks like an  undifferentiated blob cells are already  starting down the paths to their  eventual identities  but these tools researchers can also see  how development differs from one animal  to the next or even learn our recipe for  coaxing different cell types from stem  cells  [Music]
 

